WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.024 --> 00:00:02.441
(soft music)

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:14.100
- Hi, I'm Carolyn Grambling
Earth and Climate Writer

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:15.520
for Science News.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:17.360
In honor, of the 100th anniversary

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.550
of our independent news organization.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:21.540
We are interviewing leaders in science

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.640
on the big questions in their fields.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:24.840
Here we're going to be talking about

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.900
climate change communication.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.860
That can include not only
ongoing research on how best

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.310
to inoculate people against
climate science misinformation,

00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:36.180
but also how to just engage
with people on this topic

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.960
in these highly polarized times.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:41.310
My guest today is Ed Maibach,

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.500
who is the Director of The
Center for Climate Change

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.290
Communication at George Mason University.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.320
Dr. Maibach researches
public understanding

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:50.830
of and engagement in Climate Change,

00:00:50.830 --> 00:00:52.320
and looks for ways to enhance

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.080
both that understanding
and that engagement.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.650
Along with his colleague,
Anthony Leiserowitz at Yale,

00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:01.410
Dr. Maibach received the
Stephen Schneider Award

00:01:01.410 --> 00:01:05.240
for outstanding climate
science communication in 2020.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:07.870
And this is work that is
needed now more than ever.

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:10.683
So Dr. Maibach, thank you
so much for joining us.

00:01:12.350 --> 00:01:14.120
So in my introduction,

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.270
I mentioned these highly polarized times,

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:18.540
but of course that's
an oversimplification,

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:20.670
people aren't just one thing or the other.

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:24.160
And as you and your colleagues
reported about a decade ago,

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:26.310
there aren't just two
perspectives on global warming

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:27.740
in the United States.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:31.240
You identified six distinct
perspectives in the US

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:32.253
on that subject.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:36.820
- Yeah, absolutely and you
have it exactly right Carolyn

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:39.840
it's like when I started
working on climate change

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:41.543
almost 15 years ago now,

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.720
that sort of the lore out
there was that there are sort

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.780
of just two beliefs--and
I'm using that word beliefs

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.370
very intentionally here.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:53.440
It drives some of my climate
science colleagues nuts

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:56.610
when they hear me talk
about beliefs because

00:01:56.610 --> 00:01:59.210
their businesses is evidence and facts,

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:02.875
not beliefs, but
nevertheless, the rest of us,

00:02:02.875 --> 00:02:05.233
we hold beliefs about what we think,

00:02:06.836 --> 00:02:10.320
our climate scientists have concluded.

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.985
And the lore was 15 years ago that

00:02:13.985 --> 00:02:15.450
there were two types of Americans.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:18.410
Those who believed that
global warming was real

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:20.240
and human caused and serious

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.540
and those who chose not to believe that.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:26.980
And that just struck Tony and
I as pretty patently absurd.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.763
Of course there has to be
more nuanced than that.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.790
So we did something that

00:02:32.790 --> 00:02:35.470
in the marketing field they
call audience segmentation

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:37.900
analysis. It's pretty simple,

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.920
it's just using statistical data,

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.750
people's answers to our
surveys, and then running it

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:46.060
through a statistical
algorithm to look at,

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:49.010
look for people whose
answers cluster together,

00:02:49.010 --> 00:02:51.300
whose answers are similar to one another.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.490
And what we found essentially
was that there are,

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.440
six distinct groups.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.290
We call them Global
Warming's Six Americas,

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:02.210
and it's been absolutely
fascinating to watch

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:05.400
those six groups evolve over the past

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.980
we first identified them in spring of 2009

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:10.960
so we've been able to watch them evolve

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:12.283
over the past 12 years.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.070
And over the past five or six years,

00:03:16.070 --> 00:03:18.560
what's been particularly most notable

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:23.527
is that the Americans who
were least inclined to believe

00:03:23.527 --> 00:03:26.570
that human caused climate change is real.

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:28.120
We call them, in fact,

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:30.570
they're not inclined
at all to believe that

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:32.440
they're quite firm in their convictions,

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:36.940
that global warming is a scam essentially.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:40.460
And that segment of the public
about five or six years ago

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:45.090
was one out of, approximately
one out of 10, 12% exactly.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:47.220
And over the ensuing five or six years,

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:50.500
they have contracted,
they're now down to about 7%.

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:52.360
That's something we didn't expect to see

00:03:52.360 --> 00:03:56.380
because when people hold
their views strongly,

00:03:56.380 --> 00:03:59.050
they tend to hold them dearly as well.

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:01.610
Mostly they are not willing
to relinquish their views,

00:04:01.610 --> 00:04:02.960
change their views.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:05.720
But at the same time, during
that same time period,

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:08.530
the group on the other end of
the six America's continuum,

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:12.060
which we call the alarmed,
again about one out of 10,

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:14.970
five or six years ago, 12% exactly,

00:04:14.970 --> 00:04:19.720
they have grown enormously.
Well in excess of 25%.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:22.460
Now, probably we're about
to release our latest data,

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:25.260
probably three and 10, 30%.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:28.010
So there's been this enormous
growth in the proportion

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:29.630
of Americans who are concerned,

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:32.223
really deeply concerned,
about climate change.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.260
- So I'm just going to
give people a little, like,

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:37.400
since those, the six
categories that you identified,

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:38.233
you mentioned a few of them.

00:04:38.233 --> 00:04:40.900
So it goes from alarmed to concerned,

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.520
to cautious disengaged,

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:45.530
which I guess are the
people who just really don't

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:46.810
have an opinion one way or the other,

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:48.403
or feel uninformed about it.

00:04:48.403 --> 00:04:49.930
I don't know if that's accurate

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:54.369
then there's doubtful and then dismissive.

00:04:56.090 --> 00:04:59.700
And when you're doing these
surveys, like twice a year,

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.775
since 2008, when you did the
first one, is that right?

00:05:01.775 --> 00:05:02.608
- That's right.

00:05:02.608 --> 00:05:03.441
- That's why you're doing this.

00:05:03.441 --> 00:05:04.990
You're able to do this
sort of longitudinal,

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:07.260
like let's track how these
attitudes have changed.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:09.003
And I was interested to read that,

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:12.090
it wasn't just sort of like this uptick.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:14.930
It was like there was a dip
and then it went back up again

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:18.490
over time. There was a
dip around 2010, 2011.

00:05:18.490 --> 00:05:19.630
So if you can sort of talk about,

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:21.920
what are the things that
are driving those trends,

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:25.050
in people's perceptions, in your view?

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:27.210
- Yeah, that's a very good observation.

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:30.190
It hasn't been a single linear trend,

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:31.870
either upward or downward.

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:36.870
There's been a lot of
wiggling in those trend lines.

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:42.830
And the very first notable
thing that we observed

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:45.190
between our first and our second survey.

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:50.190
So between the fall of 2008
and early winter, 2010,

00:05:50.397 --> 00:05:54.580
We didn't have the
funding yet in place to do

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:55.770
surveys every six months.

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:58.253
So, it was essentially a year,

00:05:59.103 --> 00:06:01.440
a year 14 months between
the first two surveys.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:04.560
But our timing was absolutely
fortuitous in the sense that

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:07.260
we caught the high watermark

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:11.130
of public concern about climate
change in the fall of 2008.

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:13.430
If you look at Gallup polls and Pew polls,

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:16.360
polls, that came prior to ours,

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:19.860
they were showing, sort
of a long steady growth,

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:23.170
and we jumped into this polling process

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:25.500
right around the time
of the high watermark.

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:26.333
And then--

00:06:26.333 --> 00:06:27.380
- Was it like an inconvenient truth

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:28.730
was the year before, right.

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:30.970
So it was like, right around that

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:33.150
peak of interest in knowledge maybe.

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:36.640
- Yeah, an inconvenient truth
was I guess, 2006, perhaps

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:38.300
so several years prior.

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:40.940
what was actually going
on were two things.

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:42.890
I mean, a lot of things were going on,

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:44.290
but two things in particular

00:06:44.290 --> 00:06:48.600
that I think drove this dramatic downturn

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:50.420
in public concern about climate change

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:54.010
between the fall of 2008
and the winter of 2010.

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:56.830
The first one was the
financial markets melting down

00:06:56.830 --> 00:06:59.040
in America and around the world.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:00.720
So, if you recall,

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:03.840
lots of people, their
mortgages were underwater

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:05.130
they were losing their jobs.

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:06.833
It was really a terrible time.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.790
But something else important
happened at that time.

00:07:10.790 --> 00:07:13.212
And it was called the Waxman-Markey bill.

00:07:13.212 --> 00:07:18.210
And, namely, a comprehensive
climate legislation passed

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:20.690
in the US house of representatives.

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:24.290
And it was then going to,

00:07:24.290 --> 00:07:26.570
it was going to become
the job of the US Senate

00:07:26.570 --> 00:07:29.000
to essentially also ratify a bill

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:32.530
and come up with a reconciliation

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:36.090
and move comprehensive
climate legislation forward

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:37.413
to the president's desk.

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:40.770
And the president of course,
that was President Obama,

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:41.970
he would have signed it.

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:45.980
What happened in response
to the Waxman-Markey bill

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:48.890
is the climate denial machine
kicked into high gear,

00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:50.210
like ludicrous speed

00:07:50.210 --> 00:07:53.573
if I can use a Mel Brooks reference here.

00:07:55.685 --> 00:07:56.518
- Spaceballs got it.

00:07:56.518 --> 00:07:59.330
- Spaceballs thank you, exactly right.

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:02.470
So, the climate disinformation machine

00:08:02.470 --> 00:08:07.080
is incredibly well funded,
incredibly disciplined.

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:09.490
And, when they kick into ludicrous speed,

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:10.840
it's going to have an impact.

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:13.150
And in fact that's
exactly the impact we saw

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:16.050
between 2008 and 2010.

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:17.700
- And so that sort of
dovetailed with the rise

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:19.220
of the tea party, as well,

00:08:19.220 --> 00:08:22.470
as a political powerhouse I guess.

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:23.610
- Exactly, right.

00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:27.210
- Yeah, but now we're seeing
this like swing back up

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:29.140
and in fact past the point of 2008,

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:33.890
as far as the alarmed and
the concerned fraction share.

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:36.620
- That's right, so really
for the past 10 years,

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:39.670
it hasn't been a perfect
incremental increase,

00:08:39.670 --> 00:08:41.250
but for the past 10 years,

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:43.750
there has been this pretty dramatic growth

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:46.730
in public engagement, on
all of the indicators,

00:08:46.730 --> 00:08:49.560
that we are, that our research shows

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:51.570
are most important people,

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:54.800
which we boil down to by the
way, five key indicators, okay,

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:57.580
because, there are really only five,

00:08:57.580 --> 00:09:00.170
what we call essential truths

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:02.120
that when people understand them,

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:04.070
when Americans understand them,

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:08.150
it differentiates those
people who are quite concerned

00:09:08.150 --> 00:09:12.280
about the problem and are
starting to take action themselves

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:14.850
from those people who
either aren't concerned

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:16.440
or aren't taking action.

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:19.400
And those five essential
truths are its real

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:23.500
meaning global warming is real,
its' us, it's human caused

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:27.140
experts agree, there's
virtually no disagreement

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:28.740
among that climate science community

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:31.310
about the reality of human
caused climate change.

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:35.500
It's bad meaning, yes,
it's bad for plants,

00:09:35.500 --> 00:09:38.360
penguins, and polar bears,
but it's bad for people, too.

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:40.300
It's really important
for people to understand

00:09:40.300 --> 00:09:44.250
it's not just a threat
to, charismatic species

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:45.630
up in the far north.

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:48.780
But in fact, it's changing
conditions in our backyard.

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:50.920
And then finally there's hope, right?

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:55.480
So for people who feel like
the horse has left the barn,

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:57.780
there's nothing we can do about this

00:09:57.780 --> 00:09:59.510
in the words of some of my most

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:01.800
beloved climate scientist friends

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:06.020
who like to shorthand it
as we're F'd they say, no,

00:10:06.020 --> 00:10:09.560
it's true where we are
in for a world of pain,

00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:12.460
but the degree to which
we are F'd depends on

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:14.400
whether or not we respond to this.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.010
And so people who feel
like there is no hope

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:19.450
are not likely to be
rolling up their sleeves

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:21.820
and getting engaged and championing

00:10:21.820 --> 00:10:24.450
or personally engaging
in climate solutions.

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:26.510
- I have to say, when you said
there's hope those two words,

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:29.160
I immediately like took a
deep breath and went, okay.

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:34.960
So yeah, as a climate change
reporter over the last,

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:37.230
decade and a half now I guess

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:41.340
I've seen sort of this change in terms of,

00:10:41.340 --> 00:10:43.610
where people seem to be
standing in terms of,

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:45.293
denial, outright denial.

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:48.060
And for me I can sort of recognize

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:49.860
what I think of as inflection points

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:51.250
there where it's like, okay,

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:55.250
the 2018 special report from the IPCC

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:57.250
on the one and a half degree study,

00:10:57.250 --> 00:10:59.830
that seemed like a thing that
really got people's attention,

00:10:59.830 --> 00:11:03.530
suggesting that one and a half
degrees versus two degrees,

00:11:03.530 --> 00:11:06.690
there's quantifiable differences
and here's what they are.

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:10.490
And then, the youth
climate movement in 2019.

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:12.120
But your data seemed to
suggest that this change

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:14.230
was actually starting a
little bit earlier than that,

00:11:14.230 --> 00:11:18.330
that people were becoming
more engaged and more aware

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:20.790
even before 2018.

00:11:20.790 --> 00:11:21.640
So I was curious,

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:23.720
what are the things that might be driving

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:26.603
that particular tick upward?

00:11:28.670 --> 00:11:32.610
- I suspect most Americans
have paid a lot more attention

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:35.080
to the youth climate movements

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:38.180
and to young Greta Thunberg from Sweden

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:43.180
than they have paid to the
IPCC's 1.5 degree special report.

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:45.510
I personally pay a lot of attention

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:48.880
to the IPCC's 1.5 degree report

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:52.860
because it's such an important
statement that they made.

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:55.050
The statement, I'm sorry,

00:11:55.050 --> 00:11:57.600
I'm sort of going down
answering a slightly different

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:00.450
question than you asked me,
but I'm going with it anyway.

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:05.210
The reason why the 1.5 degree
report was so important

00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:10.070
is because that all
countries in the world,

00:12:10.070 --> 00:12:12.660
minus ours during the
Trump administration,

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:13.930
but now once again,

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:16.080
we're back in the Paris Climate Agreement,

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:19.570
and all countries in the world
have agreed to work together

00:12:19.570 --> 00:12:22.190
to try to limit global warming

00:12:22.190 --> 00:12:25.120
to no more than two degrees centigrade.

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:28.130
And yet they were smart enough,

00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:33.130
insightful enough to ask the
IPCC is that actually safe?

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:36.890
That two degrees turned out
to be a politically expedient

00:12:36.890 --> 00:12:40.460
number, a number that all
the nations could agree on,

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:42.660
but it really wasn't
clear as to whether or not

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:45.440
that was a safe level of warming.

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:47.800
And so they asked the
scientists of the world,

00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:51.910
in the form of the IPCC,
to do a special report

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:53.200
on exactly that.

00:12:53.200 --> 00:12:56.920
Would we be, what is the
difference for our children

00:12:56.920 --> 00:12:59.375
and our grandchildren and their
children and grandchildren

00:12:59.375 --> 00:13:03.740
etcetra, if we limit the
warming to 1.5 degrees,

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:06.930
or if we let it go all
the way up to two degrees,

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:09.550
God forbid we don't go beyond that.

00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:12.120
And what they found was that it's really,

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:13.780
the difference is quite profound.

00:13:13.780 --> 00:13:15.410
The, public health

00:13:15.410 --> 00:13:17.370
I'm a public health
professional by training

00:13:17.370 --> 00:13:20.960
so I tend to think of all of
this in public health terms,

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:23.960
the public health consequences
of letting the warming

00:13:23.960 --> 00:13:27.150
get to two degrees, as
opposed to 1.5 degrees

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:30.000
is pretty consequential,

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:33.040
I shouldn't say pretty, it
is extremely consequential.

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:36.030
There will be a lot
more public health harm

00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:38.030
if the world warms to two degrees,

00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:41.653
then if we succeed in limiting
the warming to 1.5 degree.

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:45.140
And I do think so, now coming
back to answer your question,

00:13:45.140 --> 00:13:48.090
I do think the public has paid
attention to young voices,

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:48.923
which is good.

00:13:48.923 --> 00:13:51.010
There are children,
there are grandchildren

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:52.280
we should pay attention to them,

00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:53.790
they're speaking truth to power.

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:56.420
And in fact they're
speaking science to power.

00:13:56.420 --> 00:13:59.270
I think young Greta is absolutely amazing

00:13:59.270 --> 00:14:01.473
in the way she speaks science to power.

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:06.040
But I also think that
America's health professionals

00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:08.420
have by and large started to wake up

00:14:08.420 --> 00:14:12.090
and talk about this issue,
which I think helps as well.

00:14:12.090 --> 00:14:14.390
I can tell you for a fact
that America's television

00:14:14.390 --> 00:14:16.960
weather casters have
really gotten in the game

00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:20.060
over the past five, six, seven years.

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:23.050
And so there are lots of new voices

00:14:23.050 --> 00:14:26.730
who the members of the public
weren't previously hearing

00:14:26.730 --> 00:14:29.420
who are now talking about climate change.

00:14:29.420 --> 00:14:31.800
And I think what we're seeing in terms of

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:34.900
this dramatically heightened
public engagement,

00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:37.230
I think we're seeing that the sum total

00:14:37.230 --> 00:14:40.020
of all of these new voices, who really,

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:43.920
you can say they have
ended the climate silence

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:45.610
that was raining in America

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:48.040
up until about five or six years ago.

00:14:48.040 --> 00:14:51.350
Most people simply didn't
talk about the issue.

00:14:51.350 --> 00:14:52.250
And we learn,

00:14:52.250 --> 00:14:54.880
we know that from our surveys
and we know from our surveys

00:14:54.880 --> 00:14:57.400
that most people don't hear anybody else

00:14:57.400 --> 00:15:00.370
talking about this climate
change as an issue,

00:15:00.370 --> 00:15:02.150
whether that's professionals

00:15:02.150 --> 00:15:04.520
or whether it's their friends and family.

00:15:04.520 --> 00:15:08.210
So the fact that America
has started a conversation

00:15:08.210 --> 00:15:11.250
about climate change is incredibly helpful

00:15:11.250 --> 00:15:13.950
'cause we're not likely to stand up

00:15:13.950 --> 00:15:15.610
and do anything about our problems

00:15:15.610 --> 00:15:16.443
if we're not talking about them.

00:15:16.443 --> 00:15:20.080
- Yeah, there was a scientist
I talked to back in 2019,

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:22.040
it was Kim Cobb actually at Georgia Tech,

00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:24.340
and she was mentioning like at that point

00:15:24.340 --> 00:15:26.420
after the youth climate movement,
it just felt like suddenly

00:15:26.420 --> 00:15:28.010
this was a kitchen table discussion,

00:15:28.010 --> 00:15:30.000
that it was something that
people were sitting down

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.530
and actually talking
about amongst themselves

00:15:32.530 --> 00:15:35.400
or in the grocery store
or wherever, as opposed to

00:15:35.400 --> 00:15:38.960
just sort of some rarefied distant thing.

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:41.530
And it feels like, the
extreme weather events also,

00:15:41.530 --> 00:15:45.390
which were again emphasized
by this year's IPCC report

00:15:45.390 --> 00:15:47.990
to sort of bring it home
to people that seem to also

00:15:49.293 --> 00:15:52.283
help make it more real, I think, yeah.

00:15:53.630 --> 00:15:56.990
- Yeah, absolutely, and
so climate scientists

00:15:56.990 --> 00:15:59.080
have been telling us for a very long time

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:00.920
that one of the ways that climate change

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:03.540
is going to touch our lives
first and most directly

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:05.169
is through extreme weather.

00:16:05.169 --> 00:16:10.169
And 10 years ago, when we
ask people the question,

00:16:10.650 --> 00:16:13.860
have you personally
experienced global warming?

00:16:13.860 --> 00:16:17.420
About one out of four people
would say yes to that question.

00:16:17.420 --> 00:16:21.275
Now about half of our polling respondents

00:16:21.275 --> 00:16:22.800
say yes to that question.

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:25.180
And when we asked them well in what way

00:16:25.180 --> 00:16:27.160
have you personally
experienced global warming?

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:30.150
We see very clearly that
more and more people

00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:33.130
are connecting the dots between

00:16:33.130 --> 00:16:35.820
the extreme weather
events in their community

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:37.460
and this thing that
they've been hearing about

00:16:37.460 --> 00:16:38.793
called climate change.

00:16:39.630 --> 00:16:41.170
- Yeah, I was also really struck by

00:16:41.170 --> 00:16:44.220
the most recent report that updates

00:16:44.220 --> 00:16:47.340
the six Americas showed
that the smallest fraction

00:16:47.340 --> 00:16:48.980
is now the disengaged fraction,

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:51.160
that it just goes to show
that more and more people

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:53.410
are at least coming down
on one side or the other,

00:16:53.410 --> 00:16:56.890
and, mostly on the
concerned or alarmed side.

00:16:56.890 --> 00:16:58.800
So I thought that was really telling too.

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:02.970
- There's not very, there's
fairly little demographic

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:05.990
difference between the six Americas.

00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:09.570
The disengaged segment is
the one sort of exception

00:17:09.570 --> 00:17:10.513
to that rule.

00:17:12.206 --> 00:17:14.223
They, have less household income,

00:17:15.760 --> 00:17:18.010
they're essentially a group of Americans

00:17:18.010 --> 00:17:21.030
who are working twice
as hard just to keep,

00:17:21.030 --> 00:17:23.600
keep paying the rent or
keep paying the mortgage.

00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:27.280
And so the reason they are
disengaged is not because

00:17:27.280 --> 00:17:30.560
they don't care it's
because they don't have time

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:32.110
to actually pay attention

00:17:33.008 --> 00:17:36.080
to sort of the news that had they,

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:37.350
if they did have more time,

00:17:37.350 --> 00:17:39.670
they might otherwise
be paying attention to.

00:17:39.670 --> 00:17:42.140
So, yes the disengaged segment has shrunk

00:17:42.140 --> 00:17:44.270
considerably over the past decade,

00:17:44.270 --> 00:17:46.650
and although I don't actually know

00:17:46.650 --> 00:17:48.430
the numbers off the top of my head,

00:17:48.430 --> 00:17:51.360
I can tell you, rather
than sort of inching along

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:54.070
to the next group over the
cautious and then the concern,

00:17:54.070 --> 00:17:55.160
and then the alarmed,

00:17:55.160 --> 00:17:57.250
my guess is many of them jumped straight

00:17:57.250 --> 00:17:59.800
from the disengaged segment
into the alarmed segment.

00:18:01.350 --> 00:18:04.280
- Yeah, I was going to I
wanted to ask you about,

00:18:04.280 --> 00:18:06.240
so now that you have done this work

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:08.990
to sort of know the audience,

00:18:08.990 --> 00:18:12.610
how is it helpful to develop
climate communication

00:18:12.610 --> 00:18:15.550
strategies to be able to
identify these different groups?

00:18:15.550 --> 00:18:16.950
How can you tailor different strategies

00:18:16.950 --> 00:18:18.070
to the different groups?

00:18:19.744 --> 00:18:20.990
- That's a great question.

00:18:20.990 --> 00:18:23.730
And to some degree we do exactly that,

00:18:23.730 --> 00:18:28.730
but the way, the biggest
insight for me personally,

00:18:28.870 --> 00:18:33.870
in the six Americas continuum
was the following realization:

00:18:34.420 --> 00:18:37.530
The alarmed segment, again
they've grown enormously,

00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:40.270
so when I first started thinking
about the alarmed segment,

00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:42.900
they weren't nearly as big and
important as they are today,

00:18:42.900 --> 00:18:46.090
but even then, the goal there,

00:18:46.090 --> 00:18:48.020
the climate communication objective

00:18:48.020 --> 00:18:50.720
there shouldn't be to further worry them

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:52.330
'cause they're worried enough.

00:18:52.330 --> 00:18:54.970
It should actually be
to help them understand

00:18:54.970 --> 00:18:56.940
the actions that they could take

00:18:56.940 --> 00:18:59.133
that will be really genuinely helpful.

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:03.270
And we know from our
polling that lots of people

00:19:03.270 --> 00:19:05.300
have a very, even in the alarmedsegment

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:07.550
had to have a very unclear idea

00:19:07.550 --> 00:19:11.140
about what actions are going
to be genuinely most helpful.

00:19:11.140 --> 00:19:14.580
So with regard to the alarm, we try to,

00:19:14.580 --> 00:19:16.230
and we try to help organizations

00:19:16.230 --> 00:19:19.460
who are targeting the alarmed
segment, understand, look,

00:19:19.460 --> 00:19:20.950
help these people figure out,

00:19:20.950 --> 00:19:23.650
what's most important for them to do

00:19:23.650 --> 00:19:26.060
'cause they want to take action.

00:19:26.060 --> 00:19:29.510
On the other end of the
continuum the dismissive segment,

00:19:29.510 --> 00:19:33.570
frankly we don't go after
them, we're not in the business

00:19:33.570 --> 00:19:35.400
of trying to convince people

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:37.880
who don't want to be
convinced of the truth.

00:19:37.880 --> 00:19:39.743
It's just really hard to do that.

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:43.124
And, if others,

00:19:44.049 --> 00:19:49.049
there are organizations
who are trying to debunk

00:19:49.050 --> 00:19:51.960
the misinformation that
characterizes their thinking,

00:19:51.960 --> 00:19:52.793
God bless them.

00:19:54.230 --> 00:19:56.360
That's not the way I
choose to spend my time.

00:19:56.360 --> 00:19:58.890
I choose to spend my time
on the four America's

00:19:58.890 --> 00:20:00.030
in the middle of the continuum.

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:04.480
And the reason is because, to
a greater or lesser degree,

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:07.583
they already understand that
the climate is changing.

00:20:08.777 --> 00:20:10.220
The group that we call the doubtful,

00:20:10.220 --> 00:20:12.000
which is right next to the dismissive,

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:15.130
they tend to be skeptical,
but they're still listening.

00:20:15.130 --> 00:20:17.720
They tell us very clearly in our surveys,

00:20:17.720 --> 00:20:20.140
they are still open to learning more

00:20:20.140 --> 00:20:23.030
and as long, so just because
they're a little skeptical now,

00:20:23.030 --> 00:20:24.980
as long as they're still
open to learning more,

00:20:24.980 --> 00:20:27.100
I see that as a real opportunity.

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:30.110
So the four segments in the
middle of the continuum,

00:20:30.110 --> 00:20:32.540
we have spent a fair amount of our effort

00:20:32.540 --> 00:20:34.610
trying to build communication programs

00:20:34.610 --> 00:20:36.032
that reach out to them.

00:20:36.032 --> 00:20:40.460
That help them either
understand, as I said before,

00:20:40.460 --> 00:20:42.040
that it's real, that it's us,

00:20:42.040 --> 00:20:44.380
that experts agree, that it's
bad and there are actions

00:20:44.380 --> 00:20:46.930
we can take that will make a difference.

00:20:46.930 --> 00:20:50.500
For the segments who are already inclined

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:52.500
to understand that it's happening,

00:20:52.500 --> 00:20:55.130
what our objective there
is to help them realize

00:20:55.130 --> 00:20:58.140
this isn't only a future problem

00:20:58.140 --> 00:20:59.830
this is today's problem.

00:20:59.830 --> 00:21:02.520
This problem is already
changing conditions

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:06.690
in your backyard in ways
that, sometimes are nice.

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:09.730
It's gorgeous where I
live today I went outside

00:21:10.785 --> 00:21:11.730
for a little while today,

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:14.130
and I was just stunned
at how lovely it is.

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:15.780
I understand why it's so lovely

00:21:15.780 --> 00:21:20.780
and the reason isn't good,
but it is lovely today.

00:21:21.400 --> 00:21:23.410
So we try to help people understand

00:21:23.410 --> 00:21:26.430
people who see climate
change as a distant problem,

00:21:26.430 --> 00:21:30.110
distant in space, somewhere
else, not here in my community,

00:21:30.110 --> 00:21:35.110
distant in time, 2050,
2100, yeah, but not 2021

00:21:35.370 --> 00:21:37.690
and distance and species, polar bears,

00:21:37.690 --> 00:21:40.870
but not people. We try to
help all of them realize,

00:21:40.870 --> 00:21:43.890
no, in fact, this is happening now,

00:21:43.890 --> 00:21:46.470
here, in our community, to us.

00:21:46.470 --> 00:21:47.950
And it's not good for us.

00:21:47.950 --> 00:21:51.930
And we have real strong reason
to believe from our research

00:21:51.930 --> 00:21:53.650
that when people learn

00:21:53.650 --> 00:21:55.420
those facts, they learn the ways

00:21:55.420 --> 00:21:57.610
that conditions are changing

00:21:57.610 --> 00:22:00.973
in their backyard now,
and why that isn't good.

00:22:02.110 --> 00:22:04.630
It helps to heighten
this public engagement

00:22:04.630 --> 00:22:06.925
that we've been talking about.

00:22:06.925 --> 00:22:07.820
- I was going to ask, I mean

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:10.800
I'll give you an opportunity
to critique journalists.

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:12.233
So this is obviously,

00:22:12.233 --> 00:22:14.050
I think in climate journalism,

00:22:14.050 --> 00:22:16.710
there's certainly been an increase in

00:22:16.710 --> 00:22:18.840
recognition that we need
to tell personal stories,

00:22:18.840 --> 00:22:21.800
local stories, make it, bring
it home to people in that way.

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:22.800
And I'm curious,

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:25.800
what do you think that
climate journalists like me

00:22:25.800 --> 00:22:28.040
could be doing to tell this story in a way

00:22:28.040 --> 00:22:31.700
that might actually be
impactful for that middle?

00:22:31.700 --> 00:22:36.330
- Yeah, like the old expression
that all politics is local,

00:22:36.330 --> 00:22:37.890
I tend to think that,

00:22:37.890 --> 00:22:40.670
all climate change public engagement

00:22:40.670 --> 00:22:42.600
comes from local motivations.

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:46.620
So I really do believe that
we've got, Science News

00:22:46.620 --> 00:22:50.400
does wonderful climate journalism.

00:22:50.400 --> 00:22:53.550
The New York Times, The Washington Post

00:22:53.550 --> 00:22:55.060
are absolutely outstanding.

00:22:55.060 --> 00:23:00.060
The LA Times. We have some
premier news journalistic outlets

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:03.210
that are doing outstanding
climate reporting,

00:23:03.210 --> 00:23:07.270
but I would like to see
every day news stations,

00:23:07.270 --> 00:23:11.490
both TV, radio, newspapers,
digital news outlets,

00:23:11.490 --> 00:23:13.210
I'd like to see them
covering climate change

00:23:13.210 --> 00:23:14.620
as a local issue

00:23:14.620 --> 00:23:18.000
because people can only be
expected to care so much

00:23:18.000 --> 00:23:19.830
or to pay so much attention

00:23:19.830 --> 00:23:23.110
when they learn about
what's happening elsewhere,

00:23:23.110 --> 00:23:24.930
because they're primarily interested

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:27.923
in what's happening here, in our town.

00:23:28.920 --> 00:23:31.740
So I think we have a real profound need

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:35.200
to help journalists around the
country and around the world

00:23:36.500 --> 00:23:39.880
do highly engaging
local climate reporting,

00:23:39.880 --> 00:23:42.500
contextualizing the global story

00:23:42.500 --> 00:23:44.580
in the form of local stories.

00:23:44.580 --> 00:23:46.673
What's happening here, to us, now.

00:23:47.970 --> 00:23:50.050
- So in the vein of, figuring out a way

00:23:50.050 --> 00:23:52.350
to bring the story and make it personal

00:23:52.350 --> 00:23:54.000
and relevant to individuals.

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:56.440
I know that you have this
story that you have told before

00:23:56.440 --> 00:23:59.543
about a neighbor, who you,

00:24:00.650 --> 00:24:01.890
I'm going to make you tell that story.

00:24:01.890 --> 00:24:03.388
Do you mind sharing that one again?

00:24:03.388 --> 00:24:05.043
I think it's really interesting.

00:24:05.043 --> 00:24:05.876
- I don't mind.

00:24:05.876 --> 00:24:07.210
I really ought to tell my neighbor that

00:24:07.210 --> 00:24:10.420
I'm sharing our story publicly

00:24:10.420 --> 00:24:13.090
but I actually don't think she would mind.

00:24:13.090 --> 00:24:16.540
So I've lived in this
neighborhood for a good long time,

00:24:16.540 --> 00:24:18.170
more than two decades now.

00:24:18.170 --> 00:24:21.260
And for the first decade or so,

00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:24.120
a neighbor two doors
down, her name is Nancy

00:24:25.168 --> 00:24:28.650
she felt the need to remind
me every time we talked

00:24:28.650 --> 00:24:32.032
that she is a, I'm forgetting
what kind of Republican

00:24:32.032 --> 00:24:36.282
that she is a, I'm forgetting
what kind of Republican

00:24:36.282 --> 00:24:37.290
she tells me she is,

00:24:37.290 --> 00:24:38.339
but basically she reminds me--

00:24:38.339 --> 00:24:40.335
- I think you've said,
I can just tell you,

00:24:40.335 --> 00:24:41.168
I think it was a Goldwater.

00:24:41.168 --> 00:24:43.060
- Goldwater thank you that exactly right,

00:24:43.060 --> 00:24:45.720
a Goldwater, I want to say
a Rockefeller Republican

00:24:45.720 --> 00:24:46.860
and I knew that wasn't right.

00:24:46.860 --> 00:24:50.540
A Goldwater Republican, which
is even more conservative

00:24:50.540 --> 00:24:53.410
than a Rockefeller
Republican by a long yard.

00:24:53.410 --> 00:24:56.280
And I really wasn't sure what about me

00:24:56.280 --> 00:24:58.610
that was eliciting this need in her

00:24:58.610 --> 00:25:01.350
to remind me that she is
a Goldwater Republican,

00:25:01.350 --> 00:25:05.040
but, whatever, she and I
were perfectly cordial.

00:25:05.040 --> 00:25:09.200
There was never any animosity
or strife between us,

00:25:09.200 --> 00:25:11.880
but she and my wife and
some of the other people

00:25:13.070 --> 00:25:16.440
on our block are in a
reading club, a book club.

00:25:16.440 --> 00:25:19.220
And one night, I came home from work late.

00:25:19.220 --> 00:25:23.020
I was teaching a night course
and Nancy was in my home

00:25:23.020 --> 00:25:24.820
with a bunch of other of our neighbors

00:25:24.820 --> 00:25:28.150
and Nancy wandered away from
the book club conversation

00:25:28.150 --> 00:25:29.530
and she wandered into the kitchen

00:25:29.530 --> 00:25:31.670
when I was eating the
leftovers from the book club,

00:25:31.670 --> 00:25:36.430
like, I won there, and we had a long chat

00:25:36.430 --> 00:25:38.740
and the long chat came from the fact that

00:25:38.740 --> 00:25:42.400
I remembered that she had told me

00:25:42.400 --> 00:25:45.870
that she's a vegan of three decades.

00:25:45.870 --> 00:25:49.640
And, it really hadn't occurred
to me until that night

00:25:49.640 --> 00:25:51.040
to ask her what's a good,

00:25:51.040 --> 00:25:54.600
nice Goldwater Republican like you,

00:25:54.600 --> 00:25:56.520
do with eating a vegan diet.

00:25:56.520 --> 00:25:58.610
And so I asked her, I
didn't ask her that way,

00:25:58.610 --> 00:26:00.240
but I asked her sincerely.

00:26:00.240 --> 00:26:04.680
And it launched us into the most amazing

00:26:04.680 --> 00:26:08.140
intimate conversation about
her real deep feelings

00:26:08.140 --> 00:26:09.890
about the wellbeing of animals.

00:26:09.890 --> 00:26:13.020
And she just can't bring
herself to eat animals

00:26:13.020 --> 00:26:15.820
because she feels too
much empathy for them

00:26:15.820 --> 00:26:18.780
and to want to end their lives

00:26:18.780 --> 00:26:20.030
for the benefit of,

00:26:20.030 --> 00:26:23.257
her nutritional needs--

00:26:24.460 --> 00:26:25.530
- Or desires.

00:26:25.530 --> 00:26:26.870
- Or desires, right.

00:26:26.870 --> 00:26:31.870
And that, letting her talk
to me about her motivations

00:26:32.320 --> 00:26:37.310
and what she cared
about, brought her around

00:26:37.310 --> 00:26:39.430
to asking me about my motivations

00:26:39.430 --> 00:26:40.410
and what I care about

00:26:40.410 --> 00:26:43.193
and why is it that I
work on global warming.

00:26:44.210 --> 00:26:47.350
And it gave me an opportunity
to tell her exactly why.

00:26:47.350 --> 00:26:49.130
I don't really remember the details

00:26:49.130 --> 00:26:50.360
of this part of the conversation,

00:26:50.360 --> 00:26:54.530
but I'm pretty sure I couched it

00:26:54.530 --> 00:26:57.480
in who I am as a person
and as a professional

00:26:57.480 --> 00:27:00.560
I'm first foremost, and always
a public health professional.

00:27:00.560 --> 00:27:03.270
This is how I've spent the
past four decades of my life

00:27:03.270 --> 00:27:07.000
working on behalf of
ensuring that we can all live

00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:09.103
a healthy and fulfilled lives.

00:27:10.120 --> 00:27:14.170
And explaining climate change
to her as being rooted,

00:27:14.170 --> 00:27:17.270
why I'm working on it is 'cause
I am a health professional

00:27:17.270 --> 00:27:19.250
and I can't possibly stand by

00:27:19.250 --> 00:27:23.160
and watch us destroy the climate,

00:27:23.160 --> 00:27:26.740
which is the most fundamental
determinant of human health.

00:27:26.740 --> 00:27:30.490
And being able to make my
case to her and explain it

00:27:30.490 --> 00:27:32.593
from the perspective of my values,

00:27:33.970 --> 00:27:35.840
made her comfortable enough to ask me

00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:37.840
a lot of followup questions,

00:27:37.840 --> 00:27:41.960
questions that she had in her
mind about global warming,

00:27:41.960 --> 00:27:44.360
but had never found, created

00:27:44.360 --> 00:27:46.270
the opportunity to ask anyone.

00:27:46.270 --> 00:27:48.920
But because we were having
a real moment there,

00:27:48.920 --> 00:27:51.850
she felt comfortable
asking me about the answer.

00:27:51.850 --> 00:27:53.510
Did I know the answer to these questions?

00:27:53.510 --> 00:27:54.520
And I would say,

00:27:54.520 --> 00:27:57.170
we spoke for the better part
of 45 minutes about this.

00:27:57.170 --> 00:28:00.590
And it was really a moment, I treasure it.

00:28:00.590 --> 00:28:02.400
- Yeah, I was thinking, it's,

00:28:02.400 --> 00:28:03.810
we're going into the Thanksgiving season

00:28:03.810 --> 00:28:06.700
when people are going to have
these interactions with people.

00:28:06.700 --> 00:28:07.873
And one does wonder,

00:28:08.750 --> 00:28:12.110
what are some tips that
you might recommend

00:28:13.341 --> 00:28:15.140
for creating those moments? I guess

00:28:15.140 --> 00:28:17.963
I mean, I guess we're looking
for the common ground.

00:28:18.980 --> 00:28:21.523
- Yeah, I hear that question a lot

00:28:22.793 --> 00:28:24.700
and behind the question,

00:28:24.700 --> 00:28:29.130
there's usually an assumption
and the assumption is

00:28:29.130 --> 00:28:30.683
how can I win the argument?

00:28:31.580 --> 00:28:34.390
And I think that's a really,
really bad way to think

00:28:34.390 --> 00:28:36.340
about the whole thing.

00:28:36.340 --> 00:28:40.530
And it's totally
understandable why you would,

00:28:40.530 --> 00:28:43.320
if you want to engage, right, right.

00:28:43.320 --> 00:28:45.880
You're alarmed and lots of alarmed people

00:28:45.880 --> 00:28:47.810
would like to talk to their loved ones,

00:28:47.810 --> 00:28:50.720
their neighbors, their
friends, their family,

00:28:50.720 --> 00:28:52.940
but they're worried that
it will start a fight.

00:28:52.940 --> 00:28:56.083
And they're worried that
they somehow have to,

00:28:57.210 --> 00:29:01.200
essentially be, become
mini me climate scientists

00:29:01.200 --> 00:29:04.460
or be able to channel
climate scientists to

00:29:04.460 --> 00:29:07.640
put all the facts on the
table and win the debate.

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:09.460
But that's just not the way it works.

00:29:09.460 --> 00:29:12.400
The way it works is you show
people how much you care.

00:29:12.400 --> 00:29:14.670
And, once you've shown
them how much you care

00:29:14.670 --> 00:29:18.170
and why you care, then they
might actually be interested

00:29:18.170 --> 00:29:22.070
in posing some questions
and examining some,

00:29:22.070 --> 00:29:24.690
aspects of this that they
otherwise really wouldn't be

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:27.050
in the least bit interested
in talking about it.

00:29:27.050 --> 00:29:29.930
So, what I recommend is that

00:29:29.930 --> 00:29:32.200
if you want to talk to
people about climate change,

00:29:32.200 --> 00:29:34.570
and I hope everybody
wants to talk to people

00:29:34.570 --> 00:29:36.963
about climate change, just start with,

00:29:38.300 --> 00:29:40.920
telling people how much
you care and why you care.

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:43.863
And that's likely to lead to
a really good conversation.

00:29:45.740 --> 00:29:47.920
- So it's funny as you
were mentioning the thing

00:29:47.920 --> 00:29:50.373
about wanting to win an
argument, which, fair,

00:29:51.512 --> 00:29:53.890
I'm also thinking about like the sort of

00:29:53.890 --> 00:29:55.190
research that has been going on,

00:29:55.190 --> 00:29:57.700
and I've written a little bit on climate

00:29:57.700 --> 00:30:01.450
misinformation, disinformation,
and efforts to combat that.

00:30:01.450 --> 00:30:03.950
And I'm curious, what is your,

00:30:03.950 --> 00:30:06.540
it's an interesting arena
because it brings in these areas

00:30:06.540 --> 00:30:09.610
of cognitive science and social psychology

00:30:09.610 --> 00:30:11.003
into the climate space.

00:30:12.400 --> 00:30:15.450
And I'm, there are all these like tactics

00:30:15.450 --> 00:30:18.190
that people are trying to counter

00:30:18.190 --> 00:30:19.540
with other kinds of tactics.

00:30:19.540 --> 00:30:21.720
I'm curious about what your thoughts are

00:30:21.720 --> 00:30:24.643
on how those efforts are moving forward.

00:30:26.990 --> 00:30:28.977
And you mentioned earlier,
I know that you're like,

00:30:28.977 --> 00:30:31.590
well, God bless those people for.

00:30:31.590 --> 00:30:35.341
- No, no, no, this is a
really important question.

00:30:35.341 --> 00:30:39.540
And I'm going to couch the start
of my answer in what I know

00:30:39.540 --> 00:30:42.180
as a public health
communication scientist, okay.

00:30:42.180 --> 00:30:44.483
So before I started
working on climate change,

00:30:45.827 --> 00:30:48.030
I hold a degree in public health

00:30:48.030 --> 00:30:50.000
and another degree in
communication science,

00:30:50.000 --> 00:30:51.610
and I did that very purposefully

00:30:51.610 --> 00:30:55.090
'cause I wanted my career to focus on

00:30:55.090 --> 00:30:59.453
how do we use communication
to help people,

00:31:00.620 --> 00:31:02.890
not just ordinary people,

00:31:02.890 --> 00:31:05.270
but our policymakers in particular,

00:31:05.270 --> 00:31:06.350
how do we use information

00:31:06.350 --> 00:31:08.090
to help people make better decisions.

00:31:08.090 --> 00:31:11.040
Decisions that will more reliably allow us

00:31:11.040 --> 00:31:14.020
to, more people to live
long and helpfully.

00:31:14.020 --> 00:31:18.000
And there's one and only
one thing I know for sure,

00:31:18.000 --> 00:31:21.460
in my four decades of
study on that question,

00:31:21.460 --> 00:31:26.400
and that is: every successful
public health campaign

00:31:26.400 --> 00:31:27.770
in human history,

00:31:27.770 --> 00:31:30.480
probably every successful
communication campaign

00:31:30.480 --> 00:31:32.370
of any type in human history,

00:31:32.370 --> 00:31:36.060
has succeeded on the
basis of having simple,

00:31:36.060 --> 00:31:38.790
clear messages repeated often

00:31:38.790 --> 00:31:40.783
by a variety of trusted voices.

00:31:41.860 --> 00:31:44.850
And, the trusted voices part is important

00:31:44.850 --> 00:31:49.850
because we don't necessarily
all trust the same people.

00:31:50.230 --> 00:31:54.440
So I would contend that Donald
Trump is an absolute master

00:31:54.440 --> 00:31:55.680
of simple, clear messages,

00:31:55.680 --> 00:31:58.300
repeating them often through
his own trusted voice

00:31:58.300 --> 00:32:03.300
and getting people in his milieu

00:32:03.940 --> 00:32:05.770
to repeat his simple, clear messages.

00:32:05.770 --> 00:32:09.060
And that was a very, very
powerful driving force

00:32:09.060 --> 00:32:12.600
in America over the past
four years, and still is

00:32:12.600 --> 00:32:13.483
very much so.

00:32:15.150 --> 00:32:17.940
Much of what he's saying
has no basis in truth,

00:32:17.940 --> 00:32:20.700
certainly no basis in scientific truth,

00:32:20.700 --> 00:32:22.740
but if we want for those people

00:32:22.740 --> 00:32:25.650
who are trying to communicate
scientific truths,

00:32:25.650 --> 00:32:28.240
that will help people
make better decisions

00:32:28.240 --> 00:32:30.320
we have to use that same toolkit,

00:32:30.320 --> 00:32:32.940
simple, clear messages, repeated often

00:32:32.940 --> 00:32:35.280
by a variety of trusted voices.

00:32:35.280 --> 00:32:38.280
It helps to get the repeated often part,

00:32:38.280 --> 00:32:40.680
it really helps have a lot of money.

00:32:40.680 --> 00:32:44.580
So if I am, Royal Dutch Shell or BP

00:32:44.580 --> 00:32:46.780
or ExxonMobil or Chevron,

00:32:46.780 --> 00:32:49.060
I have a lot of money to spend,

00:32:49.060 --> 00:32:52.113
to get my simple clear messages
out to the American people

00:32:52.113 --> 00:32:54.180
and people around the world.

00:32:54.180 --> 00:32:57.030
And right now, what do
I want them to know?

00:32:57.030 --> 00:33:01.330
I want them to know that we've got this.

00:33:01.330 --> 00:33:04.170
We accept the fact that
the climate is changing,

00:33:04.170 --> 00:33:06.550
but we are putting our best people

00:33:07.410 --> 00:33:10.340
on the task of creating new sources

00:33:10.340 --> 00:33:12.130
of clean, renewable energy.

00:33:12.130 --> 00:33:15.147
So, you folks don't
need to worry about it.

00:33:15.147 --> 00:33:19.570
And it's a wonderful thing for
them to tell us, to tell me,

00:33:19.570 --> 00:33:23.440
I hear it every day on
my NPR radio station,

00:33:23.440 --> 00:33:25.690
I see it in my Twitter feed,

00:33:25.690 --> 00:33:28.640
I feel like I'm surrounded 360 degrees

00:33:28.640 --> 00:33:30.900
by the fossil fuel industry telling me

00:33:30.900 --> 00:33:33.930
that they've got this thing, it's okay.

00:33:33.930 --> 00:33:36.820
But the way they do it is 'cause
they've got a lot of money

00:33:36.820 --> 00:33:38.710
to get messaged repetition.

00:33:38.710 --> 00:33:42.850
They literally pay to get
their messages in front of me,

00:33:42.850 --> 00:33:44.680
the climate science community,

00:33:44.680 --> 00:33:46.240
they don't have that kind of budget.

00:33:46.240 --> 00:33:50.160
So, and frankly, they and we

00:33:50.160 --> 00:33:52.572
are not so good at the
simple, clear messages part.

00:33:52.572 --> 00:33:55.290
- I was thinking that, yeah.

00:33:55.290 --> 00:33:57.220
- Yeah, so we have some work to do

00:33:57.220 --> 00:33:58.970
on simple, clear messages.

00:33:58.970 --> 00:34:00.840
Taking the complexity of climate change

00:34:00.840 --> 00:34:03.830
and climate solutions
and rendering it simple.

00:34:03.830 --> 00:34:05.170
We've got a lot of work to do

00:34:05.170 --> 00:34:08.580
on getting the message
repetition way up there.

00:34:08.580 --> 00:34:11.510
And if we can do those two things though,

00:34:11.510 --> 00:34:13.033
simplify our messaging,

00:34:14.060 --> 00:34:16.060
make sure that we're finding ways

00:34:16.060 --> 00:34:18.220
to get those messages out frequently,

00:34:18.220 --> 00:34:22.100
the people who are most
concerned about climate change,

00:34:22.100 --> 00:34:23.200
like you and me,

00:34:23.200 --> 00:34:26.240
we will actually incorporate
that way of thinking

00:34:26.240 --> 00:34:29.700
and those messages into
our own vocabulary,

00:34:29.700 --> 00:34:32.720
into our own dialogues with
our friends and family.

00:34:32.720 --> 00:34:35.400
And that's really the power
of simple, clear messages.

00:34:35.400 --> 00:34:38.340
'Cause the most trusted
people in all of our lives

00:34:38.340 --> 00:34:40.540
are the people who are closest to us.

00:34:40.540 --> 00:34:44.290
So it really helps when your
messages are simple and clear

00:34:44.290 --> 00:34:46.020
because members of your target audience

00:34:46.020 --> 00:34:50.120
will eventually become the
conveyors of those message.

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:53.170
So coming back to answer your question,

00:34:53.170 --> 00:34:56.090
the climate denial
industry has been changing,

00:34:56.090 --> 00:34:57.790
has been shaping public understanding

00:34:57.790 --> 00:35:00.880
on climate change for
many, many decades now.

00:35:00.880 --> 00:35:03.070
They've been talking
about it for much longer

00:35:03.070 --> 00:35:04.820
than the climate science community.

00:35:05.810 --> 00:35:10.150
So we have a very hard
job of undoing the damage

00:35:10.150 --> 00:35:13.447
that they have done in terms of shaping

00:35:13.447 --> 00:35:15.550
our understanding of climate change

00:35:15.550 --> 00:35:18.490
in ways that are
fundamentally just not true

00:35:18.490 --> 00:35:22.700
and fundamentally intended to make us

00:35:22.700 --> 00:35:24.720
not care about climate change,

00:35:24.720 --> 00:35:27.130
to make us think that
it's a distant threat,

00:35:27.130 --> 00:35:29.950
not a proximal threat.

00:35:29.950 --> 00:35:30.970
And to make us...

00:35:31.880 --> 00:35:33.410
Or that there's nothing we can do

00:35:33.410 --> 00:35:35.150
or that we should pay most attention

00:35:35.150 --> 00:35:38.890
to our own carbon footprint
which is completely ludicrous

00:35:38.890 --> 00:35:41.860
because while it is good to
reduce our own carbon footprint,

00:35:41.860 --> 00:35:43.490
we're never going to solve this problem.

00:35:43.490 --> 00:35:46.730
We're never going to keep
the warming to 1.5 degrees

00:35:46.730 --> 00:35:48.270
or even two degrees,

00:35:48.270 --> 00:35:49.440
if what we're worried about

00:35:49.440 --> 00:35:52.810
is our individual carbon footprints.

00:35:52.810 --> 00:35:55.010
What we need to worry
about is making sure ,

00:35:55.010 --> 00:35:56.980
that every nation in the world

00:35:56.980 --> 00:35:59.080
has a real climate action plan.

00:35:59.080 --> 00:36:02.920
And that that plan is focused
laser-like on limiting

00:36:02.920 --> 00:36:07.150
global warming to no more
than 1.5 degrees Celsius,

00:36:07.150 --> 00:36:10.060
which is why the upcoming
climate meeting in Glasgow,

00:36:10.060 --> 00:36:14.400
Scotland COP26 is so incredibly important.

00:36:14.400 --> 00:36:16.692
- I was about to ask you,
you're about to jet off

00:36:16.692 --> 00:36:17.611
to Glasgow, is that right?

00:36:17.611 --> 00:36:18.569
- I am.

00:36:18.569 --> 00:36:20.379
- To attend this meeting, okay.

00:36:20.379 --> 00:36:21.373
And I was going to say,

00:36:22.660 --> 00:36:25.040
what are you looking for
when you go to this meeting,

00:36:25.040 --> 00:36:25.873
what is your,

00:36:25.873 --> 00:36:28.590
this sort of is going to
segue into a future question,

00:36:28.590 --> 00:36:29.423
What gives you hope?

00:36:29.423 --> 00:36:33.110
But I guess, for now
focused on this meeting,

00:36:33.110 --> 00:36:35.030
what needs to happen there in your view?

00:36:35.030 --> 00:36:37.153
- Okay so it was a great thing,

00:36:38.050 --> 00:36:40.280
I'm actually going to start to
introduce what gives me hope?

00:36:40.280 --> 00:36:42.330
The Paris Climate Agreement gave me hope

00:36:42.330 --> 00:36:44.900
because the Paris Climate
Agreement is the first time

00:36:44.900 --> 00:36:46.670
that every nation in
the world came together

00:36:46.670 --> 00:36:48.120
and agreed on a goal.

00:36:48.120 --> 00:36:50.680
The goal they agreed to,
limiting climate change,

00:36:50.680 --> 00:36:53.580
the limiting global warming
to no more than two degrees

00:36:53.580 --> 00:36:56.190
turns out to be not such as prudent goal,

00:36:56.190 --> 00:36:57.410
but at least it's a goal, right?

00:36:57.410 --> 00:36:59.803
We got that going for us, which is nice.

00:37:00.870 --> 00:37:05.870
But, now we need to reset that
goal down to 1.5 degrees C

00:37:06.370 --> 00:37:08.800
and there are a lot of voices out there,

00:37:08.800 --> 00:37:11.670
including a lot of nations
that really aren't interested

00:37:11.670 --> 00:37:13.470
in lowering the goal,

00:37:13.470 --> 00:37:17.190
making a more ambitious goal
of limiting to 1.5 degrees C.

00:37:17.190 --> 00:37:22.190
So it's incredibly important
that as many science-based

00:37:22.190 --> 00:37:26.823
voices will be present at that meeting,

00:37:29.069 --> 00:37:33.790
fighting for that, upping
our game, going big

00:37:33.790 --> 00:37:37.550
so we can really try to limit
the warming to something

00:37:37.550 --> 00:37:42.260
that could be reasonably
considered safe for humanity.

00:37:42.260 --> 00:37:46.060
So what I'm hoping for is that countries

00:37:46.060 --> 00:37:49.360
will increase what are called their NDCs,

00:37:49.360 --> 00:37:52.050
their nationally determined contributions,

00:37:52.050 --> 00:37:55.850
no country has to do anything
it's totally voluntary.

00:37:55.850 --> 00:37:59.700
But if the people, if in my case,

00:37:59.700 --> 00:38:01.320
I am going as a health professional.

00:38:01.320 --> 00:38:03.560
So if health professionals
show up at the meeting

00:38:03.560 --> 00:38:06.790
and make it perfectly clear
to our nation's leaders

00:38:06.790 --> 00:38:08.890
who are representing us at that meeting,

00:38:08.890 --> 00:38:12.000
that we expect them to
become more aspirational,

00:38:12.000 --> 00:38:15.860
we expect them to fight hard,
not only to make bigger,

00:38:15.860 --> 00:38:17.680
more ambitious NDCs,

00:38:17.680 --> 00:38:20.410
but to push other nations to do the same.

00:38:20.410 --> 00:38:24.170
So that's what I'm hoping
will happen at COP26

00:38:24.170 --> 00:38:27.410
and, that gives me hope that
there's still the chance

00:38:27.410 --> 00:38:30.060
that we can have an impact.

00:38:30.060 --> 00:38:32.576
Every 10th of a degree of warming

00:38:32.576 --> 00:38:37.270
that we avert will pay profound dividends,

00:38:37.270 --> 00:38:39.390
profound benefits for humanity.

00:38:39.390 --> 00:38:42.780
So I don't want to pretend as
if there's something magical

00:38:42.780 --> 00:38:45.360
about 1.5 or two degrees C,

00:38:45.360 --> 00:38:49.100
but every 10th of a degree of
warming that we can prevent

00:38:49.100 --> 00:38:52.663
is going to be really a blessing
to humanity going forward.

00:38:53.630 --> 00:38:56.390
- Yeah so I guess, the flip side,

00:38:56.390 --> 00:38:59.770
this is, it's been a really
tough a couple of years,

00:38:59.770 --> 00:39:02.730
really exhausting and
grueling both due to COVID

00:39:02.730 --> 00:39:05.350
and for, extreme weather events

00:39:05.350 --> 00:39:10.010
and, having hopes sometimes feeling dashed

00:39:10.010 --> 00:39:12.150
about climate progress.

00:39:12.150 --> 00:39:16.513
So I guess, I wanted to ask first,

00:39:18.940 --> 00:39:20.850
what are your climate anxieties?

00:39:20.850 --> 00:39:22.030
What do you feel discouraged about it?

00:39:22.030 --> 00:39:24.230
And then, the flip side of that is like,

00:39:24.230 --> 00:39:25.470
how do you counter that for yourself?

00:39:25.470 --> 00:39:29.880
I guess, how do you, reach for that hope

00:39:29.880 --> 00:39:31.723
even in the middle of this,

00:39:33.850 --> 00:39:36.330
I don't know what the word is, mess.

00:39:36.330 --> 00:39:39.467
- Yeah, right now I feel
most discouraged that

00:39:40.620 --> 00:39:43.990
one possibly two US
senators are in a position

00:39:43.990 --> 00:39:47.070
to essentially put the kibosh

00:39:47.070 --> 00:39:49.870
on what otherwise would have been a really

00:39:49.870 --> 00:39:52.630
thoughtful, appropriate, ambitious,

00:39:52.630 --> 00:39:55.830
comprehensive climate package called

00:39:55.830 --> 00:39:57.883
the Build Back Better Act.

00:39:58.760 --> 00:40:01.970
It's just really perplexing to me that,

00:40:01.970 --> 00:40:05.890
I recognize that this is
obviously a hyperpolarized issue

00:40:05.890 --> 00:40:09.540
and that elected officials
want to keep their jobs,

00:40:09.540 --> 00:40:13.560
so they want to make sure
that they are not stepping out

00:40:13.560 --> 00:40:16.970
in front of and running a
foul of their constituents,

00:40:16.970 --> 00:40:20.240
but this is an
all-hands-on-deck moment here.

00:40:20.240 --> 00:40:24.120
And we have a moment in
America where we could really

00:40:24.120 --> 00:40:29.120
do what is necessary, not do
everything that is necessary,

00:40:29.710 --> 00:40:33.620
but take a major first step
towards comprehensive climate

00:40:33.620 --> 00:40:38.360
action that will make us, for
the first time, a real leader

00:40:38.360 --> 00:40:41.700
in the world. And the world
needs real leaders right now.

00:40:41.700 --> 00:40:44.510
I don't have, I don't
think Americans should have

00:40:44.510 --> 00:40:47.880
sort of that sense that the
world can't do it without us,

00:40:47.880 --> 00:40:51.520
but we are so big, our economy is so big,

00:40:51.520 --> 00:40:56.520
we have been such major
carbon polluters for so long,

00:40:56.750 --> 00:40:59.220
we have an obligation to be leaders.

00:40:59.220 --> 00:41:00.750
It's not just that we can,

00:41:00.750 --> 00:41:02.720
but we have an obligation to do it.

00:41:02.720 --> 00:41:04.860
So I'm a little bit dispirited right now

00:41:04.860 --> 00:41:07.440
that it looks like the
comprehensive climate legislation

00:41:07.440 --> 00:41:08.483
won't go through,

00:41:09.540 --> 00:41:12.490
but I also try to take the
long view and realize, okay,

00:41:12.490 --> 00:41:14.670
we'll it just means we have to work harder

00:41:14.670 --> 00:41:17.970
and hope to create enough public will

00:41:17.970 --> 00:41:19.850
to create enough political will.

00:41:19.850 --> 00:41:23.330
So that next year or the
year after, or the year after

00:41:23.330 --> 00:41:27.193
we will see the US Congress
do what is really necessary.

00:41:28.440 --> 00:41:32.600
- Well, I certainly am
reaching for that hope as well.

00:41:32.600 --> 00:41:34.720
I agree, I feel a little bit dispirited,

00:41:34.720 --> 00:41:37.150
but I'm trying to sort
of, take the long view.

00:41:37.150 --> 00:41:38.050
I think that maybe

00:41:39.320 --> 00:41:41.690
that's the only way to
sort of move forward.

00:41:41.690 --> 00:41:46.690
And, the idea of also,
trying to connect with people

00:41:46.840 --> 00:41:49.950
and do what we can to build that story

00:41:49.950 --> 00:41:52.510
and get the information out to people,

00:41:52.510 --> 00:41:55.720
even in the middle of, even

00:41:55.720 --> 00:41:57.780
and maybe not the dismissive folks,

00:41:57.780 --> 00:42:01.300
but the rest of the folks, yeah.

00:42:01.300 --> 00:42:02.790
- So to answer your question about

00:42:02.790 --> 00:42:05.630
how do I contain my own anxieties

00:42:05.630 --> 00:42:09.300
so I can continue to be
productive, I have a dear friend,

00:42:09.300 --> 00:42:10.850
she's an emergency room physician

00:42:10.850 --> 00:42:14.010
in Yellowknife, Northern
Territories, Canada,

00:42:14.010 --> 00:42:15.430
and her name is Courtney Howard.

00:42:15.430 --> 00:42:18.320
And Courtney says, I don't worry anymore

00:42:18.320 --> 00:42:21.760
I just do the work. And I love that.

00:42:21.760 --> 00:42:26.030
I love when she says that.
I wish I were like her.

00:42:26.030 --> 00:42:27.520
I do still worry,

00:42:27.520 --> 00:42:29.900
but the way I try to contain my worry

00:42:29.900 --> 00:42:32.260
so that I can continue to do the work

00:42:32.260 --> 00:42:35.000
is I've created a mental lock box.

00:42:35.000 --> 00:42:38.320
And whenever I get new information

00:42:38.320 --> 00:42:40.950
that I feel is important

00:42:40.950 --> 00:42:43.290
to update my own
understanding of where we are

00:42:43.290 --> 00:42:45.500
with regard to our changing climate

00:42:45.500 --> 00:42:50.500
and what are most attractive
solution options are

00:42:50.960 --> 00:42:55.200
I open up the box, I pay
attention to the information,

00:42:55.200 --> 00:42:58.440
at least if it's dire information,

00:42:58.440 --> 00:42:59.330
I shove it back in the box

00:42:59.330 --> 00:43:02.720
and then I slammed the box
shut and I leave it shut.

00:43:02.720 --> 00:43:05.370
And I only open it when I
need to update the information

00:43:05.370 --> 00:43:06.203
that's in there.

00:43:06.203 --> 00:43:10.480
And the rest of the time I just
focus on giving my best shot

00:43:10.480 --> 00:43:13.470
at trying to help
people, mostly Americans,

00:43:13.470 --> 00:43:16.710
but to some degree my work
is focused around the world,

00:43:16.710 --> 00:43:19.423
especially in my work
with health professionals.

00:43:20.310 --> 00:43:24.040
I try to focus on doing
my best work today.

00:43:24.040 --> 00:43:28.820
And I find that that's enormously,
I don't know, cathartic,

00:43:28.820 --> 00:43:32.730
I guess, in a sense. Staying
in the present allows me

00:43:32.730 --> 00:43:34.690
to give my best shot every day.

00:43:34.690 --> 00:43:36.450
- I was finding that
thought grounding, yes,

00:43:36.450 --> 00:43:38.010
- Yeah exactly.

00:43:38.010 --> 00:43:40.640
- Yeah, well, Dr.
Maibach, thank you so much

00:43:40.640 --> 00:43:45.320
for such an informative and
eye-opening conversation

00:43:45.320 --> 00:43:48.540
and viewers, you can learn
more about Dr. Maibach's work

00:43:48.540 --> 00:43:51.480
and keep up with the latest
research on climate change

00:43:51.480 --> 00:43:55.540
by visiting us at sciencenews.org
and connecting with us

00:43:55.540 --> 00:43:56.720
on social media.

00:43:56.720 --> 00:43:59.120
So thanks for joining
us and thank you again.

00:43:59.120 --> 00:44:00.414
- Thank you Carolyn.

00:44:00.414 --> 00:44:02.831
(soft music)

